{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/708095-good-gaming-desktops","id":708095,"title":"Good Gaming Desktops","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-10-18T20:36:01-07:00","id":708095,"title":"Good Gaming Desktops","content":"Okay, so my old reliable laptop is finally starting to show it's age, and I'm gonna need a replacement soon for the heavy-duty gaming I've put it through. Now the laptops I've looked at, holy Christ, not on my budget. But the desktops seem a little more reasonable. So does anyone here have any good suggestions for a gaming desktop?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/483-flyboy254","name":"Flyboy254","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar483_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"703"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-19T18:58:38-07:00","id":709758,"title":"","content":"It really depends, what's your budget ranger? Personally I prefer to build my desktops. It gives you the option to focus on what you want as far as processing power, RAM, video card, etc. Most semi-decent gaming desktops are in the $900 - 1200 range and they're still at the upper lower end of the spectrum. Generally you're going to be spending around 2k to build a decent gaming desktop.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3065-nist498","name":"Nist498","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3065_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"72"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-20T09:57:12-07:00","id":710828,"title":"","content":"What games do you want to play?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nist498</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/708095-good-gaming-desktops?p=709758#post709758\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Generally you're going to be spending around 2k to build a decent gaming desktop.</div> </div> </div> </div>*spit take*<br /> <br /> A 'decent' gaming desktop will set you back $1,000 at most. Yes, if you have two 980s, the latest intel processor, and a large SSD, you're looking at $2,000, but that's not 'decent,' that's 'extraordinary.' You only need one graphics card and it doesn't have to be high end. The average game doesn't use that much processor power and you're typically better off OCing an older CPU and buying a cheap watercooler.<br /> <br /> I bought a $525 desktop for my mother that runs Witcher 3 at 50 fps on a 1920x1080 monitor as long as you turn off NVidia hairworks and triscopic AA (or whatever it's called.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Come and rock me Amadeus.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/296-hippokrene","name":"hippokrene","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar296_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2399"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-20T11:30:11-07:00","id":711015,"title":"","content":"Yeah, I just put together something on Newegg that would run pretty for more than the next couple of years for 1K plus the cost of a case to put it in. $1100 if you wanna get a self-contained water-cooler for the FX-8350 CPU in it to quiet things down.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/771-starmage21","name":"Starmage21","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar771_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"65"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-20T12:25:33-07:00","id":711114,"title":"","content":"I'd go with building, as Nist suggested. It's not as difficult as it first sounds, you save a lot of money and if you have no previous knowledge you learn something new. Also, theres lots of tutorials to get you started and once you build your own PC you get a general idea of how to fix the most common problems, too.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8325-asmael","name":"Asmael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8325_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"89"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-20T16:48:27-07:00","id":711441,"title":"","content":"Also, building a PC is about as difficult as putting together a bookcase. You stick things in slots, use about 12 screws, and plug everything into your PSU.<br /> <br /> You're usually playing about $300 more for 10 minutes of someone else's labor.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Come and rock me Amadeus.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/296-hippokrene","name":"hippokrene","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar296_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2399"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-20T22:21:52-07:00","id":711818,"title":"","content":"Okay, already we're off to a good start then. So right now my main concern is being able to play Fallout 4 and XCOM 2, what would I need to be able to play those without the computer exploding on me at the bare minimum?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/483-flyboy254","name":"Flyboy254","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar483_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"703"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-21T12:12:07-07:00","id":712830,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Flyboy254</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/708095-good-gaming-desktops?p=711818#post711818\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Okay, already we're off to a good start then. So right now my main concern is being able to play Fallout 4 and XCOM 2, what would I need to be able to play those without the computer exploding on me at the bare minimum?</div> </div> </div> </div>We don't have the details for X-Com 2 yet, but the requirements for fallout 4 are here.<br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Roboto\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b>Minimum</b><br /> Windows 7/8/10 (64-bit OS required)<br /> Intel Core i5-2300 2.8 GHz/AMD Phenom II X4 945 3.0 GHz or equivalent<br /> 8 GB RAM<br /> 30 GB free HDD space<br /> NVIDIA GTX 550 Ti 2GB/AMD Radeon HD 7870 2GB or equivalent</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Roboto\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b>Recommended</b><br /> Windows 7/8/10 (64-bit OS required)<br /> Intel Core i7 4790 3.6 GHz/AMD FX-9590 4.7 GHz or equivalent<br /> 8 GB RAM<br /> 30 GB free HDD space<br /> NVIDIA GTX 780 3GB/AMD Radeon R9 290X 4GB or equivalent</span></span></span><br /> <br /> So the box would need at least 8 GB ram (if not more), Windows 7 for preference (windows 8 is lacking, and windows 10 still has issues) and a fairly new video card... I believe nvidia's new shiny stuff is currently in the 900 range for the GTX video cards, so the video requirements do not seem heavy... I'd probably aim for something in the 800 range myself. I'd probably going with an i7 processor simply because they're pretty much the default these days.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-21T13:18:39-07:00","id":712949,"title":"","content":"There isn't a GTX 800-series.<br /> <br /> The 970 and the 980s are the best cards on the market. For mid-range cards, I'd go for Radeons as GTX cards tend to have smaller buses and lower memory. I have a GTX 960, but it only has 2GB of memory, and there are times when it will under perform simply because of that. (But I got it for $100, so I can't complain.)<br /> <br /> X-Com 2 is likely to be less demanding than Fallout, so if your system can handle F4, I wouldn't worry about X2.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Flyboy254</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/708095-good-gaming-desktops?p=711818#post711818\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Okay, already we're off to a good start then. So right now my main concern is being able to play Fallout 4 and XCOM 2, what would I need to be able to play those without the computer exploding on me at the bare minimum?</div> </div> </div> </div>Can you give us a price range you're looking for?<br /> <br /> Like, are you looked for something like <a href=\"http://www.newegg.com/Product/ComboBundleDetails.aspx?ItemList=Combo.1715807\" target=\"_blank\">this?</a> It's between the minimum and the recommended.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Come and rock me Amadeus.","edited":{"userid":296,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/296-hippokrene","user":"hippokrene","date":". 10-21-2015, 04:29 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/296-hippokrene","name":"hippokrene","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar296_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2399"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-21T14:18:36-07:00","id":713012,"title":"","content":"Actually, yeah, that's perfect for what I'm looking for price wise. But what else do I need to know?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/483-flyboy254","name":"Flyboy254","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar483_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"703"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-21T15:25:51-07:00","id":713094,"title":"","content":"All I can give you on this is a few tips I learned the hard way: <br /> <br /> - Use Cable ties. Lots and lots of them. Also, be sure on having a plan of how to distribute them cables so they don't touch anything. Nothing sucks more than having to re-build the entire darn thing because something is in the way of something else. <br /> <br /> - SATA connections do not provide power. Only data. So make sure your disk and optical drivers have a power source as well as a data connection. <br /> <br /> - Do not use too much Thermal Paste. About the size of a pea is enough. The last thing you want is a giant blob of Paste clogging your fan because it couldn't spread properly. <br /> <br /> - Save every little box your stuff came in. Not only do they make for good storage during the build but you can use them once you start reselling parts. There actually is money in that. <br /> <br /> - Install the drivers for your graphics card and so on, but not from the CD they got delivered with. Those drivers are mostly outdated. Get the newest ones from the websites beforehand. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8325-asmael","name":"Asmael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8325_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"89"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}